  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-127240",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "The second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=4$ is given by\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(4)+{f}^{′}(4)(x-4)+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)(x-4)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe're given an expansion about the point $x=4,$ so the coefficient of the second-degree term is $\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4).$ In the given equation, we see that the coefficient of the second-degree term is $-5,$ so we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{″}(4)=-5\\,⟹\\,{f}^{″}(4)=-10.\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
